  "summary": "### Summary",
  "details": "### Summary\r\n\r\nEnvoy crashes if an ext_proc server sends a single gRPC message containing multiple, specially crafted `ProcessingResponse` messages. This can occur when the first response in the batch causes the gRPC stream object to be destroyed, leading to a use-after-free error when Envoy attempts to process subsequent responses in the same gRPC message.\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\nEnvoy crashes when call: callbacks_.onReceiveMessageRaw() in the function AsyncStreamImpl::onData().\r\n\r\nThe crash happens in this case:\r\n1) ext_proc server packs two unexpected responses into one gRPC message and sends to Envoy.\r\n2) Upon receiving the first `ProcessingResponse`, Envoy call `callbacks_.onReceiveMessageRaw()`.  As the `ProcessingResponse` is unexpected, this function call caused the destruction of this callbacks_ object.\r\n3) In the next iteration of the for loop, Envoy accesses the already freed `callbakcs_` object,  and crashes.\r\n\r\n### PoC\r\n\r\n1) To reproduce this issue, just configure ext_proc filter under the HTTP filter chain.\r\n2) Run the Envoy binary with trace log enabled.\r\n3) Run the ext_proc server.  Upon receiving a ProcessingRequest, it packs two ProcessingResponse messages in one gRPC message and sends back to Envoy. \r\n4) From a client VM, sends a HTTP request to Envoy which hits the ext_proc filter.\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability will cause Denial of Service (DoS). The severity is high.  It impacts all supported release versions.  Anyone who uses ext_proc in their deployment might be impacted. \r\n",
